<div class="Section1">

<p class="conf-header">2026 International Conference on Next Generation Communication & Information Processing (INCIP)<br>
Department of ICT & Instrumentation and Control, Marwadi University, Rajkot, Gujarat, India</p>

<p class="paper-title">Continuous sEMG Hand Gesture Recognition Using Attention-Enhanced Inception-BiLSTM and Class-Weighted Focal Loss: A Comparative Study with Base CNN-LSTM</p>

<div class="author-block">
    <span class="author-name">[Your Name]</span>, Enrollment No. <b>[Your Enrollment Number]</b><br>
    Department of Information and Communication Technology / Instrumentation and Control Engineering<br>
    Faculty of Technology, Marwadi University, Rajkot - 360003, Gujarat, India<br>
    Email: <i>[your_email@example.com]</i><br>
    Faculty Guide: <b>[Faculty Guide Name]</b>, Assistant Professor, Department of ICT
</div>

<div class="abstract-box">
<p class="noindent"><span class="abstract-title">Abstract</span>—Predicting hand gestures from surface electromyography (sEMG) is challenging because bio-potential signals are inherently noisy, subject-dependent, and non-stationary. In continuous acquisition streams, two physiological and computational bottlenecks arise: spatial crosstalk across adjacent forearm muscles that degrades discrimination between anatomical antagonists, and severe frame-level class imbalance (~10:1 ratio) caused by predominant resting frames (<code>noGesture</code>), which induces severe gesture under-detection under standard Cross-Entropy loss. This paper implements an improved spatial-temporal gesture classification framework following the benchmark Inception CNN-LSTM model of Barona L&oacute;pez et al. [1] on the public EMG-EPN-612 dataset, and compares four architectures on a strictly disjoint 5-participant cohort (750 training vs. 750 validation continuous sequences): a Base Inception CNN, the reference Base Inception CNN-LSTM, an Enhanced CNN with Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) channel attention, and a proposed AttnInceptionHGR architecture fusing SE channel attention, an Inception backbone, a 256-dimensional bottleneck projection, a Bidirectional LSTM, and 4-head Temporal Self-Attention under Class-Weighted Focal Loss (&gamma; = 2.0). On the held-out 750-sequence validation set, the proposed model reached <b>72.00% sequence accuracy, 72.60% Macro F1, 77.32% Macro Precision and 72.00% Macro Recall</b>, compared to 70.67%, 68.76%, 74.89% and 70.67% for the reference CNN-LSTM baseline (+3.84 percentage points gain in Macro F1). Crucially, the proposed architecture reduced active gesture sequences incorrectly classified as resting state by <b>78.26%</b> (from 92 to 20 missed gesture trials), raising resting-class precision from 57.60% to 85.92% and F1-score from 73.10% to 91.39%. Offline CPU inference latency was 53.84 ms per 24-window sequence against 49.17 ms for the baseline. We interpret the differences, trade-offs in individual gestures, and discuss limitations: a single training run, a 5-subject validation cohort, and offline batch evaluation.</p>

<p class="noindent"><span class="keywords-title">Keywords</span>—surface electromyography, hand gesture recognition, Inception network, bidirectional LSTM, channel attention, focal loss, time-series classification, myoelectric control.</p>
</div>

<h1>I. INTRODUCTION</h1>
<p>Forecasting discrete gesture intent from continuous forearm surface electromyography (sEMG) is a central problem in active myoelectric prosthetics, neuro-rehabilitation, and human-computer interaction (HCI) [1], [2]. Non-invasive surface electrodes detect compound motor unit action potentials (MUAP) generated during voluntary contractions. However, the signal-to-noise ratio is low, muscle fatigue induces non-stationary spectral drift, and cross-user anatomical variability causes models trained on one participant to degrade on unseen individuals [3], [4].</p>

<p>In continuous naturalistic interaction, two fundamental signal-processing challenges persist:</p>
<p class="noindent"><b>1. Electrode Spatial Crosstalk:</b> Compact consumer arrays (such as the 8-channel Myo Armband) record composite potentials from overlapping muscular compartments. Antagonistic gestures sharing common muscular substrates (such as <code>open</code> hand extension and <code>waveOut</code> wrist abduction) produce correlated spatial activations across adjacent channels.</p>
<p class="noindent"><b>2. Acute Resting Frame Imbalance:</b> Continuous interaction comprises brief voluntary muscle contractions (1.0&ndash;2.0 s) interspersed with prolonged resting intervals. Sliding-window segmentation across continuous trials produces an acute frame distribution wherein resting state (<code>noGesture</code>) accounts for over 68.4% of all sliding frames. Under standard Cross-Entropy loss, classifiers suffer from <i>gesture under-detection</i>, erroneously predicting active muscle contractions as resting state.</p>

<p>Barona L&oacute;pez et al. [1] proposed combining an Inception Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) with an unrolled Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) network and a modal majority post-processor on the public EMG-EPN-612 dataset, reporting 90.55% &plusmn; 9.45% recognition accuracy across 306 test subjects. This is the base paper of the present study. Two key questions follow from it. First, can integrating Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) channel attention dynamically recalibrate electrode channels and resolve spatial crosstalk between antagonistic muscle groups? Second, does introducing a 256-dimensional bottleneck projection resolve the 53&times; parameter bloat of the reference paper's uncompressed LSTM (22,464 input features) while enabling bidirectional temporal modeling and Class-Weighted Focal Loss to mitigate resting-state gesture under-detection in low-data regimes?</p>

<p>The comparison is summarised in Table I. All implemented models share identical signal preprocessing, sliding windowing (1.5 s window, 0.15 s stride, 24 frames/trial), and STFT spectrogram generation (24-point Hanning window, 13 frequency bins from 0 to 12 Hz), so differences in results originate from the architectural learner, loss formulation, and temporal attention mechanisms rather than data preconditioning.</p>

<p class="table-caption">TABLE I. BASE PAPER AND IMPLEMENTED APPROACHES</p>
