</tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>The contributions of this paper are:</p>
<p class="noindent">&bull; <b>(a)</b> a leakage-controlled, participant-independent pipeline in which 5 training participants (750 sequences) and 5 validation participants (750 sequences) have strictly zero overlap (Train &cap; Val = &empty;) with per-trial normalization;</p>
<p class="noindent">&bull; <b>(b)</b> an implementation and empirical evaluation of four distinct sEMG architectures (Base Inception CNN, Base Inception CNN-LSTM, Enhanced Inception CNN, and Proposed AttnInceptionHGR) under identical training and evaluation settings;</p>
<p class="noindent">&bull; <b>(c)</b> an 88.6% compression of the recurrent input dimension (22,464 &rarr; 256), halving parameter overhead while introducing bidirectional recurrence and multi-head temporal self-attention;</p>
<p class="noindent">&bull; <b>(d)</b> a technical discussion of gesture-specific trade-offs, showing that Class-Weighted Focal Loss reduces missed active gestures misclassified as resting state by 78.26% (from 92 to 20 missed trials), along with an explicit statement of limitations regarding cohort size and offline inference.</p>

<p>The remainder of the paper is organised as follows: Section II describes the methodology, Section III presents and discusses the experimental results, and Section IV concludes.</p>

<h1>II. METHODOLOGY</h1>

<h2>A. Overall Research Methodology</h2>
<p>The workflow comprises seven sequential steps: (1) obtain the public EMG-EPN-612 benchmark dataset; (2) clean, normalize, rectify, and filter the raw 8-channel sEMG signals; (3) segment continuous trials into 24 overlapping sliding frames of 1.5 s each; (4) extract 24-point STFT spectrograms across 13 discrete frequency bins (0&ndash;12 Hz); (5) split samples into strictly disjoint training and validation cohorts by participant ID; (6) train four model architectures using validation-based early stopping; and (7) evaluate sequence-level performance with Mode 1-1 modal post-processing.</p>

<h2>B. Implemented Framework</h2>
<p>Fig. 1 illustrates the end-to-end framework. All four models share Steps 1 to 5, ensuring that evaluation is conducted on exactly the same 750 validation sequences.</p>

<div class="code-block">
Raw 8-Channel sEMG Signal (fs = 200 Hz, 5.0 s Trial, 1,000 Samples)
                      │
                      ▼
[ Data Preprocessing: ADC Normalization (/128.0) + Absolute Rectification |x[n]| ]
[ 5th-Order Digital Butterworth Low-Pass Filter (fc = 10 Hz, filtfilt zero-phase) ]
                      │
                      ▼
[ Sliding Windowing: L = 300 samples (1.5 s), step = 30 samples (0.15 s stride) ]
[ STFT Spectrogram Generation: 24 points, 13 frequency bins (0 to 12 Hz) ]
                      │
                      ▼ Continuous Sequence: (T=24, Channels=8, Freq=13, Time=24)
┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                        Model Implementation Stage                            │
│  - Algorithm 1: Base Inception CNN (Spatial only, 135k params)               │
│  - Algorithm 2: Base Inception CNN-LSTM (Base Paper Reproduction, 11.65M)    │
│  - Algorithm 3: Enhanced Inception CNN (SE Attention + Focal Loss, 5.75M)    │
│  - Algorithm 4: Proposed AttnInceptionHGR (SE + Inception + 256-d Proj +     │
│                 BiLSTM + 4-Head Temporal Attention + Focal Loss, 6.05M)      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
                      │
                      ▼ Sequence Frame Logits: (T=24, Classes=6)
[ Loss Optimization: Class-Weighted Focal Loss (gamma=2.0, label smoothing eps=0.05) ]
                      │
                      ▼
[ Temporal Modal Post-Processor: Mode 1-1 Dominant Threshold Voting ]
                      │
                      ▼
Final Sequence-Level Gesture Prediction: {fist, open, pinch, waveIn, waveOut, noGesture}
</div>
<p class="fig-caption">Fig. 1. Workflow of the implemented continuous sEMG gesture recognition framework.</p>

