<h2>D. Data Pre-processing and Feature Extraction</h2>
<p><b>Cleaning and Normalization:</b> Raw 8-channel signals are inspected for transmission dropouts. If max |x| &gt; 1.0, signals are divided by 128.0 (half-scale of 8-bit ADC). Full-wave absolute rectification |x[n]| is calculated across all channels to obtain muscular energy envelopes.</p>

<p><b>Butterworth Low-Pass Filtering:</b> A 5th-order digital Butterworth filter with normalized cutoff W<sub>n</sub> = 0.10 (f<sub>c</sub> = 10 Hz) is applied along the time axis using zero-phase forward-backward filtering (<code>scipy.signal.filtfilt</code>), exactly reproducing the filter order and cutoff frequency of [1].</p>

<p><b>Sliding Windowing:</b> Continuous 5.0-second trials (1,000 samples) are partitioned into sliding frames of length L = 300 samples (1.5 s) with step size &Delta;L = 30 samples (0.15 s stride), producing T = 24 overlapping frames per trial.</p>

<p><b>STFT Spectrogram Representation:</b> For each 300-sample frame, a 24-point Short-Time Fourier Transform with 50% overlap (step = 12) is evaluated across 13 frequency bins (0 to 12 Hz) using a periodic Hanning window:</p>

<div class="equation">
<i>w</i>[<i>n</i>] = 0.5 &middot; (1 &minus; cos(2&pi;<i>n</i> / <i>N</i>)), &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <i>n</i> &isin; {0, 1, ..., 23} &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (1)
</div>

<p class="noindent">The discrete Fourier basis matrix is computed as:</p>

<div class="equation">
<i>W</i><sub><i>k,n</i></sub> = exp(&minus;<i>j</i> &middot; 2&pi; <i>f<sub>k</sub> n</i> / <i>f<sub>s</sub></i>), &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; <i>f<sub>k</sub></i> &isin; {0, 1, ..., 12} Hz &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (2)
</div>

<p class="noindent">Each frame yields a tensor <b>X</b><sub><i>t</i></sub> &isin; &real;<sup>8 &times; 13 &times; 24</sup> (Channels &times; Frequencies &times; Time steps), forming continuous sequence tensors <b>S</b> &isin; &real;<sup>24 &times; 8 &times; 13 &times; 24</sup>.</p>

<p><b>Participant-Independent Partition:</b> The 10 participants are partitioned into strictly disjoint subsets (Train &cap; Val = &empty;). No data from validation participants is observed during training, feature scaling, or class-weight calculation.</p>

<h2>E. Algorithm Description</h2>

<h3>1) Algorithm 1 &ndash; Base Inception CNN</h3>
<p>Reproduces the spatial feature extractor of Barona L&oacute;pez et al. [1]. The 6-module Inception backbone processes each frame independently. Features are flattened (72 &times; 13 &times; 24 = 22,464) and mapped directly to 6 classes via a linear layer without temporal recurrence. Trained using standard Cross-Entropy loss.</p>

<h3>2) Algorithm 2 &ndash; Base Inception CNN-LSTM (Base Paper Reproduction)</h3>
<p>Exact PyTorch re-implementation of the base paper architecture [1]. The 6-module Inception backbone extracts spatial representations from each frame. The raw output (22,464 dimensions) is unrolled into a unidirectional LSTM with 128 hidden units, followed by a linear classification head (128 &rarr; 6) and standard Cross-Entropy loss. This model incurs 11.65 million learnable parameters (53&times; the parameter count of the CNN).</p>

<h3>3) Algorithm 3 &ndash; Enhanced Inception CNN</h3>
<p>Augments Algorithm 1 with Squeeze-and-Excitation (SE) channel attention across the 8 electrode channels, a dense projection head (Linear 22,464 &rarr; 256, LayerNorm, ReLU, Dropout 0.30), and Class-Weighted Focal Loss.</p>

<h3>4) Algorithm 4 &ndash; Proposed AttnInceptionHGR (Enhanced CNN-LSTM)</h3>
<p>Our primary proposed architecture comprises six integrated stages:</p>
<p class="noindent"><b>1. SE Channel Attention:</b> Squeezes each frame <b>X</b> &isin; &real;<sup>8 &times; 13 &times; 24</sup> via spatial average pooling <i>z<sub>c</sub></i> = (1 / (<i>FW</i>)) &sum;<sub><i>i</i>=1</sub><sup>13</sup> &sum;<sub><i>j</i>=1</sub><sup>24</sup> <i>X</i><sub><i>c,i,j</i></sub>, and gates electrode channels via:</p>

<div class="equation">
<b>s</b> = &sigma;(<b>W</b><sub>2</sub> &middot; ReLU(<b>W</b><sub>1</sub> <b>z</b>)) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (3)
</div>

<p class="noindent">where <b>W</b><sub>1</sub> &isin; &real;<sup>4 &times; 8</sup>, <b>W</b><sub>2</sub> &isin; &real;<sup>8 &times; 4</sup>, and &sigma;(&middot;) is the sigmoid activation. Calibrated input: <b>X&#771;</b> = <b>s</b> &odot; <b>X</b>.</p>

<p class="noindent"><b>2. Inception Backbone:</b> 6 multi-scale blocks (1&times;1, 3&times;3, 5&times;5, maxpool) with residual additions joining modules 1a&ndash;1c and 1c&ndash;1e, outputting 72 feature maps and 2D Batch Normalization.</p>

<p class="noindent"><b>3. Bottleneck Dimensionality Projection:</b> Compresses 22,464 flattened features to 256 dimensions:</p>

<div class="equation">
<b>h</b><sub><i>t</i></sub> = Dropout(ReLU(LayerNorm(<b>W</b><sub><i>p</i></sub> <b>f</b><sub><i>t</i></sub> + <b>b</b><sub><i>p</i></sub>)), <i>p</i>=0.30) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (4)
</div>

<p class="noindent">where <b>W</b><sub><i>p</i></sub> &isin; &real;<sup>256 &times; 22,464</sup>, reducing recurrent input dimensions by 88.6%.</p>

<p class="noindent"><b>4. Bidirectional LSTM:</b> A 2-layer BiLSTM (hidden = 64 per direction, total 128) captures past and future temporal context across the 24 time steps:</p>

<div class="equation">
<b>H</b><sub><i>t</i></sub> = [&rarr;<b>h</b><sub><i>t</i></sub> &parallel; &larr;<b>h</b><sub><i>t</i></sub>] &isin; &real;<sup>128</sup> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (5)
</div>

<p class="noindent"><b>5. Temporal Attention:</b> A 4-head self-attention module attends to contraction bursts:</p>

<div class="equation">
Attention(<b>Q</b>, <b>K</b>, <b>V</b>) = softmax(<b>Q</b> <b>K</b><sup><i>T</i></sup> / &radic;<i>d<sub>k</sub></i>) <b>V</b> &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (6)
</div>
<div class="equation">
<b>Z</b> = LayerNorm(<b>H</b> + MHA(<b>H</b>, <b>H</b>, <b>H</b>)) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (7)
</div>

<p class="noindent"><b>6. Class-Weighted Focal Loss:</b> Formulated as:</p>

<div class="equation">
&Lscr;<sub>Focal</sub> = &minus;&sum;<sub><i>c</i>=1</sub><sup>6</sup> &alpha;<sub><i>c</i></sub> (1 &minus; <i>p</i><sub><i>t,c</i></sub>)<sup>&gamma;</sup> log(<i>p</i><sub><i>t,c</i></sub>) &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; (8)
</div>

<p class="noindent">with focusing parameter &gamma; = 2.0, label smoothing &epsilon; = 0.05, and class weighting &alpha;<sub><i>c</i></sub> computed dynamically from training batch frequencies.</p>

