<h2>D. Discussion of Results</h2>
<p><b>Reduction of Active Gestures Misclassified as Resting State:</b> In the baseline CNN-LSTM, 92 active gesture sequences (20 <code>fist</code>, 13 <code>open</code>, 49 <code>pinch</code>, 9 <code>waveIn</code>, 1 <code>waveOut</code>) were erroneously misclassified as <code>noGesture</code>, caused by Cross-Entropy loss succumbing to the 68.4% resting frame dominance (precision = 57.60%). Our proposed model reduced these active gesture sequences misclassified as <code>noGesture</code> to <b>20</b> (a <b>78.26% reduction in missed gestures</b>), raising resting-class precision to 85.92% and F1 to 91.39% (+18.29 percentage points). Concurrently, 3 actual resting trials were misclassified as <code>pinch</code> (slight false activation during rest, yielding 97.60% recall vs. 100.0% in the baseline).</p>

<p><b>Resolution of Crosstalk vs. Class-Specific Trade-offs:</b> For <code>waveOut</code>, the proposed model achieved <b>100.0% precision</b> (0 false positives), completely preventing erroneous wrist extension commands during other actions. However, <code>waveOut</code> recall dropped from 97.60% to 69.60% due to 34 trials being misclassified as <code>open</code>, reflecting remaining shared extensor activation. For <code>pinch</code>, recall doubled from 34.40% to 68.80% (F1: 47.25% to 51.65%), but precision was constrained to 41.35% due to false positives from <code>fist</code> (50 trials) and <code>open</code> (58 trials) due to partial finger flexion overlap.</p>

<p><b>Inference Latency and Real-Time Considerations:</b> On a quad-core CPU, average offline inference latency was 53.84 ms per continuous sequence, comfortably below the 100&ndash;125 ms controller delay threshold cited in prosthetic literature [14], [15].</p>

<p><b>Limitations:</b> (1) The evaluation uses 5 training subjects and 5 validation subjects; absolute accuracies cannot be compared directly with the 90.55% reported on 306 subjects in [1]. (2) Single-run execution without confidence intervals. (3) Offline non-causal processing (bilateral context and whole-trial filtering).</p>

<h1>IV. CONCLUSION</h1>
<p>This paper implemented and compared four sEMG gesture recognition architectures based on the benchmark of Barona L&oacute;pez et al. [1]. By introducing Squeeze-and-Excitation channel attention, a 256-dimensional bottleneck projection, a Bidirectional LSTM, and Class-Weighted Focal Loss, the proposed <code>AttnInceptionHGR</code> architecture achieved <b>72.00% sequence accuracy</b> and <b>72.60% Macro F1</b> on the EMG-EPN-612 validation cohort, outperforming our re-implementation of the baseline CNN-LSTM (+3.84 percentage points Macro F1) and cutting missed active gestures misclassified as resting state by <b>78.26%</b>. Future work will expand training across the full 306-subject cohort, report per-window recognition accuracy, and develop causal online models for embedded edge microcontrollers.</p>

